package com.ascent.climb.data.i18n

/**
 * Cloud-reading lessons (m26 / t22) question bank.
 * Split out of Quiz3: a single mapOf with this many long strings
 * overflows the 64 KB method limit and fails the Kotlin compiler.
 */
internal val PACK_EN_QUIZ_CLOUD: Map<String, String> = mapOf(

    "q.m26#0.p" to "The cloud in the photo has already formed an anvil top. What is the right move now?",
    "q.m26#0.o0" to "Keep climbing and reassess at camp",
    "q.m26#0.o1" to "Stop climbing now, leave the ridge and other exposed ground, and prepare to retreat",
    "q.m26#0.o2" to "The weather is improving, so continue to the summit as planned",
    "q.m26#0.o3" to "Wait on the spot for an hour to see whether it dissipates",
    "q.m26#0.e" to "An anvil means the cloud has punched through the 0 °C level and reached maturity: violent updraughts, ice crystals spreading out, squalls and lightning. From anvil to thunderstorm often takes only tens of minutes, and there is no shelter up high. Descend and get off the ridge and isolated high points instead of staying up there to watch.",
    "q.m26#1.p" to "A smooth lenticular cloud appears above the snow peaks. What does it tell you most clearly?",
    "q.m26#1.o0" to "Very strong wind aloft, with mountain waves",
    "q.m26#1.o1" to "Heavy snow is imminent",
    "q.m26#1.o2" to "The air is stable and the weather will stay fine",
    "q.m26#1.o3" to "A local thunderstorm is forming",
    "q.m26#1.e" to "Lenticular clouds are standing-wave clouds that form as air crosses a ridge: they barely move and look like lenses or flying saucers. They produce almost no precipitation, but they mean strong winds aloft and heavy turbulence near the ridge. Do not linger on exposed ground, and re-evaluate the summit plan.",
    "q.m26#2.p" to "Rows of small cloud patches appear at mid level (a mackerel sky). What does it usually mean?",
    "q.m26#2.o0" to "The weather will deteriorate within 12 to 24 hours",
    "q.m26#2.o1" to "It will certainly pour today",
    "q.m26#2.o2" to "Light winds aloft and stable weather",
    "q.m26#2.o3" to "Fog has already formed at ground level",
    "q.m26#2.e" to "Rows of altocumulus mean warm, moist air is rising slowly at mid levels and moisture is increasing. It does not rain by itself, but it often appears a day or two ahead of an approaching system, which is the moment to reinforce the camp and think through the retreat route.",
    "q.m26#3.p" to "The sun is veiled but its outline is still clear. What does this layer usually mean?",
    "q.m26#3.o0" to "Only a little ice crystal aloft; nothing will change",
    "q.m26#3.o1" to "A warm front is approaching; precipitation is likely within a day",
    "q.m26#3.o2" to "Surface temperature is about to jump",
    "q.m26#3.o3" to "It will certainly be a sunny day",
    "q.m26#3.e" to "Cirrostratus is the leading edge of a warm front: it arrives as a thin veil, then thickens and lowers into altostratus and nimbostratus, and precipitation begins. When you see it, start planning on the conservative side instead of waiting for rain to fall.",
    "q.m26#4.p" to "Pouch-like shapes hang from the cloud base. What is the correct reading?",
    "q.m26#4.o0" to "Severe convection has passed or is decaying, but squalls and lightning may remain",
    "q.m26#4.o1" to "The weather has fully cleared; break camp and go",
    "q.m26#4.o2" to "This marks the end of precipitation",
    "q.m26#4.o3" to "It is only an optical illusion",
    "q.m26#4.e" to "Mammatus forms on the underside of cumulonimbus and usually means the storm is weakening or has just passed. The danger has not gone with it: squalls, lightning and further precipitation can continue. Do not rush off; check the wind and the state of the team first.",
    "q.m26#5.p" to "A uniform grey sheet swallows the summit. What should you guard against first?",
    "q.m26#5.o0" to "Excessive ultraviolet",
    "q.m26#5.o1" to "A sudden loss of visibility and of direction",
    "q.m26#5.o2" to "Precipitation will immediately turn into a downpour",
    "q.m26#5.o3" to "The temperature will rise quickly",
    "q.m26#5.e" to "Stratus is thin and produces little precipitation, but when it covers the mountain the direct consequence is falling visibility: route markers, landmarks and teammates can vanish within tens of metres. Close up the spacing, check bearing and time often, and stop to wait if needed.",
    "q.m26#6.p" to "High up, small white cloud flakes with no shading. What does this tell you?",
    "q.m26#6.o0" to "A stable moist layer aloft: no change soon, but watch whether it thickens",
    "q.m26#6.o1" to "A thunderstorm is already overhead",
    "q.m26#6.o2" to "A gale is about to hit the ground",
    "q.m26#6.o3" to "The temperature will drop ten degrees",
    "q.m26#6.e" to "Cirrocumulus sits above 5,000 m and is made of ice crystals; the flakes are tiny and show no shading. It brings no precipitation and does not mean a change is imminent. The real signal is what follows: sheets of altostratus and a base that keeps lowering. Treat it as a note that the system is still far away, not an alarm.",
    "q.m26#7.p" to "The sun has turned into frosted glass with a blurred edge and almost no shadow on the ground. What does this layer mean?",
    "q.m26#7.o0" to "The cloud has thickened into mid level and keeps lowering; precipitation may start within hours",
    "q.m26#7.o1" to "The weather is clearing; go for the summit",
    "q.m26#7.o2" to "Just a thin high layer, no impact",
    "q.m26#7.o3" to "Strong wind aloft but no moisture",
    "q.m26#7.e" to "Altostratus is the second stage of a warm-front sequence: cirrostratus, then altostratus, then nimbostratus. A blurred sun with no shadow means the layer is already thick and holds plenty of water. Compress the summit into a short window, waterproof the kit and pick the retreat line now, instead of waiting for the first flakes.",
    "q.m26#8.p" to "A deep grey sheet keeps snowing with no edge in sight. What is the reasonable call?",
    "q.m26#8.o0" to "Steady continuous precipitation that may last hours or all day: drop the summit bid and switch to hypothermia and avalanche management",
    "q.m26#8.o1" to "It will certainly clear within half an hour",
    "q.m26#8.o2" to "This is a thunderstorm precursor, lie down at once",
    "q.m26#8.o3" to "Just local mist, keep to the plan",
    "q.m26#8.e" to "Nimbostratus forms when a whole layer is lifted slowly: the precipitation is continuous, visibility poor and there is no shower pattern. The main risk is not lightning but wet snow, whiteout, and fresh-snow avalanche plus hypothermia. Drop the summit, return to camp or descend, and put warmth and avalanche judgement first.",
    "q.m26#9.p" to "Rolls of low cloud split the sky, with blue showing through the gaps. What matters most now?",
    "q.m26#9.o0" to "The height of the base and the top, which decide whether you are under the cloud or above it",
    "q.m26#9.o1" to "Whether thunder is coming",
    "q.m26#9.o2" to "How strong the UV is",
    "q.m26#9.o3" to "Whether the temperature will rise above zero",
    "q.m26#9.e" to "Stratocumulus usually means a stable layer with little or no precipitation. What it decides is whether you are under the cloud or above it: if the base sits below your route, the ridge will be swallowed and visibility will collapse; if the top stays below the summit, the view above is clean. Compare base height with your high point before setting out.",
    "q.m26#10.p" to "Small cumulus with flat bases and rounded tops appear in the morning. What does that mean?",
    "q.m26#10.o0" to "Convection has just started and the weather is still fine, but watch whether the tops keep growing",
    "q.m26#10.o1" to "It will certainly rain today",
    "q.m26#10.o2" to "Strong wind aloft, no summit attempt",
    "q.m26#10.o3" to "Fog is about to form in the valley",
    "q.m26#10.e" to "Fair-weather cumulus, with tops flattened by an inversion. Read the trend: if an hour later the tops are still flat and the clouds are small and scattered, the layer is stable; if they start bulging upward and the bases darken and flatten, convection is building and the afternoon may bring cumulonimbus. This is the earliest call of the day.",
    "q.m26#11.p" to "In the afternoon this cloud keeps punching upward and its edges turn hard and sharp. What is most likely next?",
    "q.m26#11.o0" to "It grows into a cumulonimbus, with lightning and violent gusts within one to two hours",
    "q.m26#11.o1" to "It will dissolve on its own soon",
    "q.m26#11.o2" to "It turns into continuous snowfall",
    "q.m26#11.o3" to "The shape is stable, nothing will change",
    "q.m26#11.e" to "Towering cumulus is convection in its teens: the top is still rising and the edges are crisp. The storm is not mature yet, but once the top starts to fan into an anvil and the edges blur, the dangerous phase has begun. This is the last unhurried moment to decide whether to continue or turn back; do not wait for thunder.",
    "q.m26#12.p" to "A round hole opens in a mid or high cloud sheet, with threads falling from its edge. What does it tell you?",
    "q.m26#12.o0" to "The layer holds supercooled droplets below 0 °C, so the ridge will ice up",
    "q.m26#12.o1" to "The cloud is dissolving and the weather is improving",
    "q.m26#12.o2" to "Heavy precipitation is imminent",
    "q.m26#12.o3" to "Nothing to do with weather, just an optical effect",
    "q.m26#12.e" to "A fallstreak hole forms when supercooled droplets in the layer freeze after a disturbance; the surrounding vapour sublimates and falls, leaving a hole. For a climber the point is not the hole but the proof that the layer is below freezing yet still holds liquid water: ridges, ropes and rock will rime up, and both grip and protection are affected.",
    "q.m26#13.p" to "At dawn the valley is filled with a flat band of fog while the summits are already in sun. What does that mean?",
    "q.m26#13.o0" to "Classic radiation fog, usually lifting and clearing after sunrise; the fog top is a useful height reference",
    "q.m26#13.o1" to "Strong convection is building in the valley",
    "q.m26#13.o2" to "It will rain all day",
    "q.m26#13.o3" to "Strong wind aloft is pushing down",
    "q.m26#13.e" to "Overnight cooling saturates the valley floor and forms radiation fog, which lifts and clears as the sun warms it. It tells you two things: the valley is humid and feels colder, and the fog top often sits close to the inversion, so it works as a reference for cloud-base height. If you are not in a hurry, waiting for it to lift gives you a far better view.",
    "q.m26#14.p" to "A dark lowering with a fairly sharp edge hangs from the cloud base and rotates slowly. What is it?",
    "q.m26#14.o0" to "A wall cloud under a strong updraft, meaning the storm is at its peak; leave the high ground at once",
    "q.m26#14.o1" to "An ordinary low cloud, keep going",
    "q.m26#14.o2" to "A sign of coming snowfall",
    "q.m26#14.o3" to "Valley fog lifting",
    "q.m26#14.e" to "A wall cloud is the lowering under a severe storm's updraft, often with rotation and a strong downdraft nearby. In mountains it means you are close to the storm core: extreme gusts, hail and frequent lightning are all possible. Do not keep climbing, and do not shelter on the lee side of the ridge; descend and get off the high ground.",
    "q.m26#15.p" to "At dusk the cloud top is lit by the setting sun and spreads outward in fibres. What does that mean for tomorrow?",
    "q.m26#15.o0" to "Today's convection matured and moisture is still aloft, so reassess the window in the morning",
    "q.m26#15.o1" to "Tomorrow will certainly be clear",
    "q.m26#15.o2" to "Tomorrow will certainly be wet",
    "q.m26#15.o3" to "Just sunset colour, nothing to do with weather",
    "q.m26#15.e" to "A fibrous, spreading top is the anvil of a cumulonimbus, made of ice crystals; it often survives for hours after sunset. It shows there is still moisture and instability aloft. That does not guarantee a bad day, but it does rule out reusing today's assessment: check base height, visibility and wind again before you set off.",
    "q.m26#16.p" to "Low, ragged cloud fragments race across the sky with a messy base. What do they usually mean?",
    "q.m26#16.o0" to "Strong low-level wind and turbulent flow, often with gusts or precipitation; no lingering on ridges",
    "q.m26#16.o1" to "The weather is improving",
    "q.m26#16.o2" to "The air is still, almost no wind",
    "q.m26#16.o3" to "Fog is about to form in the valley",
    "q.m26#16.e" to "Fractus, also called scud, is low cloud torn apart by strong wind, typically near a front or gust front. It produces no precipitation itself, but it proves strong low-level wind and turbulence; on ridges and cols it shows up as a gust with no warning, strong enough to take you off your feet. Shorten the time you spend on exposed ground to the minimum.",
    "q.m26#17.p" to "Several white trails hang high up for a long time and slowly spread into a sheet. What does that indicate?",
    "q.m26#17.o0" to "Plenty of moisture aloft; a weather system may be approaching",
    "q.m26#17.o1" to "Very dry air aloft; the weather will stay fine",
    "q.m26#17.o2" to "A gale is coming at ground level",
    "q.m26#17.o3" to "A thunderstorm is imminent",
    "q.m26#17.e" to "How long a contrail lasts depends on temperature and humidity aloft: in dry cold air it vanishes quickly, in a moist layer it stretches, spreads and stays for hours. Persistent, spreading trails mean plenty of moisture up there, often the opening of a cloud sequence. It is not a forecast, but it belongs next to thickening cirrus and a lowering base.",
    "q.t22#0.p" to "In the afternoon a tall tower shoots up over the valley and its top begins to fan out. What do you do?",
    "q.t22#0.o0" to "Hurry over the col before the rain arrives",
    "q.t22#0.o1" to "Leave ridges, water and isolated trees; move lower and into the trees",
    "q.t22#0.o2" to "Keep to the plan and deal with it after the rain",
    "q.t22#0.o3" to "Pitch a tarp in an exposed spot",
    "q.t22#0.e" to "A tower that grows fast and fans into an anvil marks mature convection; lightning and short heavy showers usually follow within 30 to 60 minutes. Ridges, water, isolated trees and open ground carry the highest lightning risk: get away from them first, then think about shelter.",
    "q.t22#1.p" to "At dawn a flat sheet of cloud covers the lake and the low ground, though the road is still visible. How do you plan the day?",
    "q.t22#1.o0" to "Set off as usual, it is cooler walking in fog",
    "q.t22#1.o1" to "Wait for it to lift, and have warm layers, light and spare time ready",
    "q.t22#1.o2" to "Cancel the whole route",
    "q.t22#1.o3" to "Take the path by the water, the fog will be thinner there",
    "q.t22#1.e" to "Ground-hugging stratus and advection fog form after overnight cooling and lift as the sun warms the ground. The problem is not the cold but the loss of both visibility and judgement: junctions, waymarks and partners all blur. An hour of waiting is usually cheaper than an extra hour in fog; check warm layers and headtorch while you wait.",
    "q.t22#2.p" to "The storm has just passed and pouches hang from the cloud base. What should you least do now?",
    "q.t22#2.o0" to "Break camp at once and make up the time the rain cost you",
    "q.t22#2.o1" to "Stay in camp, check the kit and drainage, and wait for the base to lift",
    "q.t22#2.o2" to "Note how the wind has shifted",
    "q.t22#2.o3" to "Put wet clothing into a dry bag",
    "q.t22#2.e" to "Mammatus usually appears as a storm decays, but decaying is not safe: gusts, lightning and rain upstream are all still there. On foot the real hazard is the lag: one to three hours after the rain stops, creeks and flash floods reach their peak. Rushing off puts you at the worst possible moment to cross.",
    "q.t22#3.p" to "This thick grey sheet with no visible edge has been drizzling all morning. What most needs reassessing?",
    "q.t22#3.o0" to "Creek levels and crossing points, and whether to switch to a high traverse",
    "q.t22#3.o1" to "Whether you have enough sun protection",
    "q.t22#3.o2" to "Whether thunder is coming today",
    "q.t22#3.o3" to "Whether visibility will improve",
    "q.t22#3.e" to "Nimbostratus brings continuous rain: modest intensity, long duration. The practical threat is the accumulated water raising the streams; a creek you hopped across on stones at breakfast may be knee deep by afternoon. At every crossing, judge how fast the level is rising before you commit.",
    "q.t22#4.p" to "This arc of cloud with a ragged skirt is pushing across the water and the wind has picked up. What comes first?",
    "q.t22#4.o0" to "Get off the water and the open bank at once, into lee-side trees or a hollow",
    "q.t22#4.o1" to "Speed up and cross before the cloud arrives",
    "q.t22#4.o2" to "Stand on the bank and take a few photos",
    "q.t22#4.o3" to "Crouch where you are and wait for it to pass",
    "q.t22#4.e" to "This is a shelf cloud on the leading edge of a gust front: the cloud moves at the speed of the gust, usually with a sudden wind shift and a short burst of strong wind. Water and open bank offer neither shelter nor protection from falling trees. Crossing ahead of it is the worst option, because the gust arrives while you are in the water with no margin left.",
    "q.t22#5.p" to "This low cloud wedge is advancing from the distance, its base neat and its top dark. What usually follows it?",
    "q.t22#5.o0" to "Strong gusts first, rain later; the wind is the bigger danger",
    "q.t22#5.o1" to "Steady light rain straight away",
    "q.t22#5.o2" to "A rapid rise in temperature",
    "q.t22#5.o3" to "The sky clearing for good",
    "q.t22#5.e" to "A shelf cloud belongs to a gust front: cold air pouring out lifts the warm moist air into a wall of cloud. It arrives as a violent gust and a pressure jump, with rain following behind. Prepare for wind rather than rain: secure the tarp and anything light, keep the group together, and stay away from isolated trees.",
    "q.t22#6.p" to "For two days in a row the sky shows these broad sheets of mackerel cloud. What does it mean?",
    "q.t22#6.o0" to "Moisture is increasing at mid levels and a system is approaching: shorten the day and note the escape routes",
    "q.t22#6.o1" to "Just an effect of the light",
    "q.t22#6.o2" to "The air is very dry and the weather stable",
    "q.t22#6.o3" to "It will certainly rain tomorrow",
    "q.t22#6.e" to "A mackerel sky means more moisture at mid levels and a system drawing closer. It does not guarantee rain, but it is enough to tilt the plan to the cautious side: shorten the day's goal, note the escape routes, and do not camp on a gravel bar or in a valley bottom. Two days running deserves more weight than a single sighting.",
    "q.t22#7.p" to "Dusk brings a sky burnt red like this. What is the most reasonable reading for tomorrow?",
    "q.t22#7.o0" to "There is cloud and moisture at mid and high levels: look again in the morning instead of trusting the glow",
    "q.t22#7.o1" to "Tomorrow will certainly be clear",
    "q.t22#7.o2" to "Tomorrow will certainly rain",
    "q.t22#7.o3" to "Sunset colour only reflects air quality",
    "q.t22#7.e" to "The old saying about red skies holds in some situations: high cloud to the west with a clean low level means the bad weather is still far off. But it only works if you can actually see the western horizon and the cloud really is moving east. Mountain systems change fast, so the reliable move is to re-read base height, wind and visibility next morning.",
    "q.t22#8.p" to "The way ahead is closed off by this low wall of cloud, its base close to the ground. What should you do?",
    "q.t22#8.o0" to "Stop and judge base height and wind direction before entering",
    "q.t22#8.o1" to "Push through quickly, it may be clear on the other side",
    "q.t22#8.o2" to "Camp right here and wait for it to pass",
    "q.t22#8.o3" to "Climb higher to see how big it is",
    "q.t22#8.e" to "A cloud wall sitting on the ground means extremely high humidity at low levels, visibility about to collapse, and usually a gust front as well. Walking into it means giving up your ability to navigate; climbing for a look puts you in the strongest wind and closest to the lightning. Stop, work out its direction and speed, then choose between detour and wait.",
    "q.t22#9.p" to "One side of the ridge carries cloud piled up by the terrain while the other is blue. What does that tell you?",
    "q.t22#9.o0" to "The windward slope forces air up into cloud, the lee side sinks and clears; over the top it turns drier and warmer",
    "q.t22#9.o1" to "Both sides have exactly the same weather",
    "q.t22#9.o2" to "Heavy rain is coming on the lee side",
    "q.t22#9.o3" to "The whole mountain is raining",
    "q.t22#9.e" to "This is orographic cloud: air is forced up the windward slope and condenses, then sinks and warms on the far side, clearing to leave a rain shadow. In practice the windward side is damp, cold, murky and more likely to rain, while the lee is drier, warmer, sunnier and short of water. Pack and plan water for the side you will actually be on after the col.",
    "q.t22#10.p" to "A round hole appears in the mid-level sheet with threads falling from its edge. What does it mean for camping tonight?",
    "q.t22#10.o0" to "The layer is below freezing yet still holds liquid water, so ground and kit will frost and ice overnight",
    "q.t22#10.o1" to "It will certainly rain tonight",
    "q.t22#10.o2" to "A gale is about to start",
    "q.t22#10.o3" to "Hail will fall out of the hole",
    "q.t22#10.e" to "A fallstreak hole proves there are supercooled droplets in the layer; when disturbed they freeze and the vapour falls away. For camping the practical effect is that this layer is below zero, so overnight radiative cooling will frost the tent, the sleeping bag surface and boardwalks. Keep the mat and dry clothing sealed, and watch for slick planks and stones in the morning.",
    "q.t22#11.p" to "How should these fine, hair-like high clouds be read?",
    "q.t22#11.o0" to "Their appearance alone means rain",
    "q.t22#11.o1" to "They are fair-weather cloud; only thickening and densifying signals a change",
    "q.t22#11.o2" to "They mean strong wind at ground level",
    "q.t22#11.o3" to "They mean a sudden drop in temperature",
    "q.t22#11.e" to "Cirrus is made of ice crystals high up and is common in fine weather. What matters is the trend: sparse threads gradually thickening, joining into a sheet and lowering usually mean a system is approaching; if they stay sparse, the effect is small.",
)
